  "question": {
    "text": "<p>The intensity of <math><mi>γ</mi></math> - radiation from a given source is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>I</mi></math>. On passing through <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>36</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>mm</mi></math> of lead, it is reduced to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac bevelled=\"true\"><mi>I</mi><mn>8</mn></mfrac></math>. Assuming that the intensity in transmitted radiation varies exponentially with the thickness of the material, then the thickness of lead, which will reduce the intensity to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac bevelled=\"true\"><mi>I</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>, will be&nbsp;</p>\r"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#xA0;</mi><mi>mm</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>9</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#xA0;</mi><mi>mm</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>18</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#xA0;</mi><mi>mm</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
